{"entryId":"1591","paragraphs":[{"sourceId":"heilprin:sefaria-warsaw:ta:1591:1","sourceSha256":"0c11808971c430481fa9172571fbc70915aa990b5097d5fcb89097fb7c471005","englishSha256":"e8fdc79f0c01f441ffacb360c3da233e2671a94e3253df05453dec3be13cb723","citations":[{"id":"1591:1:citation-01","he":[16,26],"en":[23,50],"target":{"url":"https://www.sefaria.org/Bereshit_Rabbah_41.4"},"reference":"Bereshit Rabbah 41:4","assessment":"The midrash gives Rabbi Tuvyah bar Yitzchak and Rabbi Tuvyah bar Rabbi Yitzchak within the same passage. The English preserves the received shorter Tuvi.","evidence":[{"reference":"Bereshit Rabbah 41:4","target":{"url":"https://www.sefaria.org/Bereshit_Rabbah_41.4"},"witness":"Midrash Rabbah -- TE"}]},{"id":"1591:1:citation-02","he":[28,35],"en":[52,70],"target":{"url":"https://www.sefaria.org/Eruvin_28b.21"},"reference":"Eruvin 28b:21","assessment":"Rabbah bar Tuvyah bar Yitzchak is explicit in Eruvin and in the reciprocal Heilprin notice. Preserve the separate title and spelling of the names in their own witnesses.","evidence":[{"reference":"Eruvin 28b:21","target":{"url":"https://www.sefaria.org/Eruvin_28b.21"},"witness":"William Davidson Edition - Vocalized Aramaic"},{"reference":"Seder HaDorot 3151:1","target":{"entry":3151,"paragraph":1},"witness":"Pinned Warsaw digital witness"}]}],"inlineHebrew":[0,1],"holds":[],"notes":[{"text":"The Bereshit Rabbah form and Rabbah patronymic in Eruvin support the two pieces of evidence separately. Preserve the heading’s form.","references":[],"urls":[]}],"comparisonEvidence":[{"reference":"Bereshit Rabbah 41:4","target":{"url":"https://www.sefaria.org/Bereshit_Rabbah_41.4"},"witness":"Midrash Rabbah -- TE"},{"reference":"Eruvin 28b:21","target":{"url":"https://www.sefaria.org/Eruvin_28b.21"},"witness":"William Davidson Edition - Vocalized Aramaic"},{"reference":"Seder HaDorot 3151:1","target":{"entry":3151,"paragraph":1},"witness":"Pinned Warsaw digital witness"}]}],"entryEvidence":[]}
